          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state, in answer to his request to say which daughter loves him most. The focal words are therefore a clear avowal, regardless of whether they are sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression asserts present love but does not promise future conduct, undertake a new duty, or renew specified obligations. Lear's subsequent territorial grant follows the full declaration, but it does not turn “I love you” itself into an undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"This is a solicited, rhetorically developed answer in a formal contest, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged verbal response. Its extravagance does not by itself establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.82,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression functions moderately as competitive ceremonial praise and self-presentation intended to satisfy Lear's test and secure his bounty. This instrumental, encomiastic function is not fully captured by T, P, or E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to declare which loves him most, linking their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. Goneril, ordered to speak first, gives an extended and hyperbolic declaration of filial love; the focal expression occurs at its opening.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril undertakes no specific action or future obligation through the focal words. Lear rewards the completed declaration by assigning her and Albany territory, but this is uptake of her performance in his love test, not acceptance of a promise contained in “I love you.”\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare's King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage establishes the utterance's avowal and strategic setting but does not settle Goneril's actual affection or sincerity. Cordelia's comparative claims about her own love do not by themselves prove that Goneril feels none.\"}"
